{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514316-v20-caitiff-question","id":1514316,"title":"v20 caitiff question","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-09T09:56:37-07:00","id":1514316,"title":"v20 caitiff question","content":"If i understand correctly, caitiff can - for unkown reason - manifest disciplines at will. Which means they can have any set of disciplines at start, but can they also learn new disciplines without a teacher or blood required?<br /> <br /> Is there a limit like bloodline based say Mortis or Mytherica or Daimonion, Obtenebration or could they have access to these too?<br /> I guess learned disciplines like thaumaturgy, koldunism, necromancy etc. are out as everyone has to learn those intelectually?<br /> <br /> Is there any lore explanation on why caitiff can randomly manifest disciplines? Only thing i found was that the last vampires to be known to be able to do this are the antedeluvians. is that a reason why they are so feared?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4364-orkar","name":"Orkar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4364_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T10:24:55-07:00","id":1514318,"title":"","content":"Complicated question<br /> Caitiff can purchase any Discipline at character creation, but thereafter have to pay six times current rating for any and all powers purchased with experience points. Even could access &quot;exotic&quot; ones, tho' the Storyteller have the final word, but in theory? The could have anthing - manifestation of Obeah, Mytherica, Daimonion and such are porbably result in a quick death mind you<br /> Necromancy Mortis, any form of Blood Magic are more of a &quot;have an affinity&quot; thing, even Clans with those have to learn and study them - but with proper story and background? Could work. <br /> <br /> These vampires have no inherent clan weakness, but no inherent disciplines as well. None of the typical clan markers apply to them - While Caitiff are Embraced normally by existing clans and bloodlines across the gamut of Kindred existence, something seems to happen between sire and childe. Either certain defining traits are not passed along in the Embrace, or some manner of imprinting is missing. As a result, the childe inherits none of their sire's clan's disciplines or weaknesses.<br /> Terms &quot;Caitiff&quot; and &quot;Thin-blooded&quot; are often considered synonymous. While there is considerable overlap, not all Caitiff are thin-bloods.<br /> The appearance of Thin-blooded have done nothing to soothe the fears of the Elders and many are more happy when there is a Caitiff less to prowl the night. Many cite the Book of Nod and claim that the blood has weakened to the point on which it can no longer establish the clan lineage. The appearance of the special Caitiff-like vampires known as Thin-bloods is also a growing concern and many Elders decide to wipe away every vampire without Clan protection.<br /> <br /> Many Caitiff in ancient times speculated that they are indeed the original vampires, heirs to the legacy of the Second Generation and Caine himself, because of the lack of ties - and could achieve <i>anything.</i> They are the manifestation, the quintessence of Caine's blood with all the potential<br /> <br /> The problems is, Vampire is a bit inconsistent... depending of the supplement even an abandoned childe would normally manifest the marks and weaknesses of their own clan. <br /> However, by swearing fealty to an elder of renown, it is possible that some Caitiff become adopted by a Clan. Many of those Caitiff are Blood bound and develop characteristics of the Clan they are bound to due to the regular infusion with foreign vitae....<br /> <br /> Then there is the Stoneman<br /> The Stoneman is a legendary Caitiff who is rumored to be the oldest one in existence. <br /> Some stories claim that he has successfully diablerized an Antediluvian. Others say that he is a lost member of the Second Generation who has traveled into the Orient. He expresses first-hand knowledge of Carthage, the Anarch Revolt, and similar events in Kindred history. Rumors are that he developed a host of unique disciplines on his own and is willing to teach them to those clanless that meet his standards.<br /> <br /> In short, they are so feared because they are anomalies and their existence is a threat to everthing the Cainite society built on; Clans, characterisitcs, Low or High status, origin stories, source of power, curses and weaknesess... and Cainites not really handling that well<br /> The scariest thing the Vampires could face is the dreadfull question: &quot;What if we were wrong all along...?&quot; - then everything crumbles around them and the existence of Caitiffs are basically that question manfiest...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T10:25:17-07:00","id":1514319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the character is a Clanless Caitiff, she may purchase<br /> whatever Disciplines she wants, <b>subject to Storyteller<br /> approval</b> </div> </div> </div>So basically, whatever the ST decides.<br /> <br /> When deciding how I think they work, I started by looking at the Setite Warriors. The bloodline is specifically created by taking a normal Setite fledgling and training them in Potence instead of Obfuscate. That's literally it. You teach a baby vampire a different discipline while they're growing up and they have it at in-clan costs.<br /> Implication; while they may be easier to learn due to blood ties, disciplines are a matter of nurture over nature.<br /> <br /> So, if that fledgling was abandoned, and nobody taught them their clan disciplines, they may well develop different ones. If they've a bossy personality, and/or are used to giving orders, they may develop Dominate instead. If they survive by going unnoticed, they're probably still going to develop Obfuscate. Since they're self-taught, the discipline is harder to learn than it would be if they'd been raised by a sire or other mentor, but since they know how to learn them on their own, they're capable of working out other disciplines too.<br /> <br /> Exceptions being blood sorcery, which aren't quite the same thing as disciplines and absolutely do require a teacher (or an infernal pact).\u200b I would also would not easily allow esoteric disciplines like, for example, Obtenebration, simply because that's a result of the Lasombra's innate ties to the Abyss. A Caitiff might be able to reverse engineer such specialty disciplines, and establish their own connection to the Abyss, but I think that would be an ability earned during a session if not an entire chronicle. It would also end with the Caitiff manifesting the No Reflection flaw to go with their shiny new discipline.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T12:01:48-07:00","id":1514327,"title":"","content":"It's held ambiguous, but there are hints (ie it's an ST option) that Clan, Curse, and native Discipline spread are an instance of consensual- and/or naming-magic. Most prominently the ability to swap them by identifying as a different bloodline:<br /> While you can explain Clan offshoot bloodlines with an elder who created progeny after mastering a foreign Discipline, what happens with Baali Apostates, Caitiff, and Panders, or even synthetic bloodlines like Tremere, Nagaraja, and Gargoyles makes it very likely the whole thing is a magic property attached to the Name of the Clan.<br /> <br /> For a Caitiff this means they simply do not know their Clan-Name and can not identify as such. Thus, if you subscribe to this interpretation, they have no set Curse or Discipline spread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T12:31:08-07:00","id":1514332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514316-v20-caitiff-question?p=1514327#post1514327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's held ambiguous, but there are hints (ie it's an ST option) that Clan, Curse, and native Discipline spread are an instance of consensual- and/or naming-magic. <br /> ...<br /> For a Caitiff this means they simply do not know their Clan-Name and can not identify as such. Thus, if you subscribe to this interpretation, they have no set Curse or Discipline spread.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could be, the theory have a lots of merit supported by the factm Caitiff are actually not traitless Vampires, and their pre-conceptions about Vampires have a tendency to influence them somewhat.<br /> This not only influences strengths and weaknesses, but is the canon explanation for the disciplines they have when sired.<br /> If they <i>believe</i> vampires are &quot;supposed&quot; to be physically far superior to mortals, they'll start with Celerity, Potence, and Fortitude.<br /> If they believe Vampires are seducing sex gods, they'll start with Presence and/or Dominate...<br /> This is as close as one could get to Paradigm vs. Consensus as a vampire<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T01:51:10-07:00","id":1514428,"title":"","content":"it does sound somewhat logical if you look at mages paradigms but i think this theory falls flat, due to first, vampires loosing their avatar and thus the ability to do something with a paradigm as the tremere prove, second, Tremere could have manifested more magical disciplines, say necromancy, thaumaturgy, dur an ki - all of which the order of hermes surely would have some knowledge on.<br /> <br /> Also, while there is NPCs like this malkavian that believes to be dracula and manifested Tzimisce disciplines instead of malkavian ones, it is a super rare exception and bloodlines are usually changed by something supernatural like the kyasid or blood brothers which causes the new discipline to appear.<br /> <br /> Finally vampires are said to be very static creatures, barely changing, so them having the ability to will themselves into developing new things seems a bit counter to the narrative BUT there ARE lore examples for it happening.<br /> <br /> Maybe there is a little caitiff in all of us,and it is that the weaker the blood becomes, the more of your inner caitiff is unleashed, just like you loose blood points and lower the maximum of dots you can have on something, the thinner the blood the more your inner caitiff appears. Afaik the youngest lore caitiff are 8th generation but are way more likely to be caitiff at 12th and later, until at 15th you are always caitiff if im not mistaken.<br /> <br /> Maybe those clan vampires that did spontainiously manifest new disciplines didnt do so as spontainiously as they think and there were supernatural shenannigans going on they are not aware of, or in case of a malkavian, they simply forgot like he may have diablerised a tzimisce as the fiends occasionally do, and just forget that he didnt always have animalism and viscissitude<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4364-orkar","name":"Orkar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4364_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T03:39:05-07:00","id":1514440,"title":"","content":"Partly, Caitiff have no &quot;In-Clan&quot; Disciplines. No Discipline is any more or less innate to them. They're as close to &quot;factory settings&quot; Vampirism as you can get in someone who isn't Caine.<br /> <br /> For this reason, other factors have far greater control over which Disciplines develop. The vampiric condition, despite being metaphysically tied to Stasis, is more mutable than we often give it credit for. A newly Embraced vampire can develop a Flaw that makes them allergic to garlic or repelled by holy symbols, seemingly for no other reason than because they <i>believe</i> that's how vampires work. Their preconceived notions about vampires gives them very real Flaws.<br /> <br /> Stands to reason, then, that this works to the Caitiff's benefit at times. In most vampires, &quot;the Blood remembers&quot;, as I like to say. In Caitiff, the Blood doesn't remember so well. So the Caitiff's beliefs about what vampires should be able to do dictate what powers manifest, or which ones they seek to cultivate.<br /> <br /> While from a Storyteller perspective I'm inclined to limit starting access to many rare, Clan-specific Disciplines, if only for balance, I do think Caitiff randomly developing them makes for good plots. What's more threatening to the status quo than a clanless whelp who spontaneously develops strong supernatural powers?<br /> <br /> The exception is blood sorcery, which works very differently to normal Disciplines. Blood magic is basically a hack, grafting a highly symbolic system onto the vampiric condition in order to exploit the powers of Vitae. You can't just cast spells, you have to commit to the sorcerous (un)lifestyle. You have to speak the works, make the signs, gather the materials, and observe the forms and bans. The particulars are wide open, but they all involve injecting &quot;The Art&quot; into it. Regular Disciplines just...work. They come naturally to vampires, whereas blood sorcery must always be learned.<br /> <br /> A newly Embraced vampire is only going to start with blood magic if they had a teacher post-Embrace, or if they were <i>already</i> a Sorcerer/Mage pre-Embrace. This has nothing to do with being a Caitiff, and everything to do with having a body of occult knowledge upon which to base developing skill with blood magic.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T04:01:05-07:00","id":1514446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Orkar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514316-v20-caitiff-question?p=1514428#post1514428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">it does sound somewhat logical if you look at mages paradigms but i think this theory falls flat, due to first, vampires loosing their avatar and thus the ability to do something with a paradigm as the tremere prove, second, Tremere could have manifested more magical disciplines, say necromancy, thaumaturgy, dur an ki - all of which the order of hermes surely would have some knowledge on.<br /> <br /> Also, while there is NPCs like this malkavian that believes to be dracula and manifested Tzimisce disciplines instead of malkavian ones, it is a super rare exception and bloodlines are usually changed by something supernatural like the kyasid or blood brothers which causes the new discipline to appear.<br /> <br /> Finally vampires are said to be very static creatures, barely changing, so them having the ability to will themselves into developing new things seems a bit counter to the narrative BUT there ARE lore examples for it happening.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it's never explicitly stated, so the Naming magic theory is just that - a theory. However, it may have more legs than appears at first.<br /> First though: Paradigm, Consensus, and True Names are not concepts exclusive to Mages, so having an Avatar or not doesn't really factor into the equation I think. They are - together with Identity - universal concepts that appear in virtually all expressions of magic.<br /> <br /> The Tremere are actually a good example. As an artificial blood line, they do not inherit the Curse from the poor sods they used to make themselves, because they created a new Name. Beckett spends a whole episode in the <i>Jyhad Diary </i>to figure out how a bloodline is able to detach itself from the Curse of their (alleged) parent Clan, which should not be possible. The Panders have a literal Renaming ritual that detaches new convert members from the mystical connections to their Clan. The Apostate ritual of the Baali works similarly, and even replaces the native Disciplines of the convert - both replace their Clan identity with a new one.<br /> <br /> As for Disciplines, if the affinity for them would be strictly hereditary, every childe would inherit it from their sire. That is obviously not the case, as every Caitiff demonstrates, so there must be another mechanism behind it. In an older version (I don't remember which) of the game, Caitiff even used to be able to create their own Disciplines. This concept was later reworked into thin-blooded alchemy, vampires so far removed from their origins that their connection to it gets fuzzy. Again, there is a nice conversation between Beckett and a thin-blooded &quot;Malkavian&quot; in <i>BJD</i> about this, and how identity rules over what you are and can do.<br /> <br /> Naming magic is of course just <i>one</i> way to explain all this, but it fits rather neatly as a theory.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T22:52:00-07:00","id":1514550,"title":"","content":"Dr. Douglas Netchurch in Beckett's Jyhad Diary had a few theoris regarding the Thin-blood and the Caitiff, he called <b>Thin Blood Syndrome</b><br /> &quot;While we call the condition \u201cthin blooded,\u201d this is a misnomer. The blood of neonates and thin-blooded is no \u2018thinner\u2019 than that of elders. Indeed, the physical qualities remain the same. What seems to cause the weakening of blood is a less efficient use. Elders achieve more potent results with the same amount of blood. Additionally, I found that while an average neonate can use all of her blood, thin-blooded are unable to. A portion of their vitae remains unresponsive when called upon to fuel so-called \u201cpowers,\u201d heal wounds,or raise physical qualities. Their blood is not useless however, as I have found it diminishes upon rising: the \u201cuncooperative\u201d vitae is still used to fuel the reanimation of the body, but no more.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Thin Blood Syndrome exhibits in higher Generations, rising from a minimal occurrence in the 12th to universality in the 15th. We find a similar increase, though never reaching universality, with the Caitiff condition. It would be erroneous to say one leads to the other however. Rather, I believe the thin-blooded and Caitiff conditions are symptoms of the same underlying cause: a too-large distance from the Clan founders.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Patient Zero \u2014 or \u201cCaine\u201d \u2014 and his childer were \u201cuniversal\u201d Kindred, but we see different powers and weaknesses in the Third Generation. Furthermore, we see a similarly abrupt evolution in bloodlines. I believe Caitiff and thin-blooded are another such leap in our evolution. To wit, I mark these differences between the thin-blooded species and our own: minor resistance to sunlight (though not staking or other sources of damage, including fire,) a minor talent for prophecy, and a small aptitude in creating new powers. The latter in particular, has led me to believe we are dealing with renewed speciation. The Caitiff are the first step, as they lose their Clan characteristics. The thin-blooded are the next step: they gain new characteristics.&quot;<br /> <br /> Also, the <b>Cainite Strain </b>theory of his is also interesting<br /> <br /> &quot;I\u2019ve not yet been able to pinpoint the source of our condition. Based on symptoms, I believe we are infected with a spiritual virus passed down through blood. Unlike living viruses, this spiritual strain is unable to mutate and is rendered weaker in each new host. We may have reached the end of the virus\u2019 viability in thin-blooded, as they cannot fully maintain the Cainite state.&quot;<br /> <br /> The problem of <b>Resumed Metabolism </b>itslef present an interesting wellspirn of theories<br /> <br /> &quot;I have documented the growth of hair and nails, returned mortal perspiration and tears. I also found traces of white blood cells in one subject\u2019s vitae and original DNA in another\u2019s. To ensure this was not pre-Embrace blood, I fully exsanguinated both subjects. Studying their vitae immediately after, I found no traces of white blood cells or original DNA, yet both returned a month later. Lastly, three subjects experienced the return of menstrual periods or virile seminal fluids, allowing them to theoretically conceive mortal children called Dhampirs. The infection of non-Kindred by the Cainite strain has been documented before, from gestated hosts (creating ghouls) to unborn embryos (creating Revenants.)&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;The brief emergence of mortal metabolism in thin-blooded yields an interesting perspective on the Cainite condition. Traditional belief holds that Cainite bodies are dead while the virus reanimates us. If that were the case, however, the lapses in thin-blooded should result in them being dead for short moments. That they instead <i>resume</i> mortal functions, indicates our bodies are alive and the condition keeps them in a suspended state. However, the nature of the thin-blooded Embrace contradicts this. While a normal Embrace takes only moments, a thin-blooded might experience hours or days between receiving vitae and reanimating. During this time, she is effectively dead. I find this contradiction intriguing and have begun to document attempted thin-blooded Embraces.&quot;\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-11T04:24:10-07:00","id":1514568,"title":"","content":"I think in times of Thin Blood there was written something that they can usually have the common one plus Protean.<br /> <br /> I would guess Anarch Thaumaturgy would also pretty easy to justify.<br /> <br /> I think with everyting else it should really depend on the Backstory.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5199-aahz","name":"Aahz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}